# Assembly Architecture: Core / Gameplay / Presentation

Company projects split runtime code into three assemblies with one-way references, enforced by asmdefs:

| Assembly | Folder | May reference |
|----------|--------|---------------|
| `<Game>.Core` | `Runtime/Core/` | Unity engine only — shared infrastructure |
| `<Game>.Gameplay` | `Runtime/Gameplay/` | Core, contracts, networking — **not** presentation |
| `<Game>.Presentation` | `Runtime/Presentation/` | Core, Gameplay, Unity UI/TMP |

**Direction:** Presentation → Gameplay → Core. Never the reverse — the compiler enforces it; don't fight the asmdef by moving files to the wrong layer.

## What goes where

- **Core** — game-local infrastructure (e.g. that game’s `SingletonBehaviour<T>`). Shared catalog/loading lives in `MonkeyTilt.Shared.AssetCatalog`; the Shared singleton base is `SharedSingletonBehaviour<T>` in `MonkeyTilt.Shared.Core`. No gameplay state, no presentation, no networking.
- **Gameplay** — client game state, server sync, session flow, player actions, the game-state singleton exposing state + events + commands. No `Canvas`, TMP, view classes, overlays, or scene presentation code. No spawning presentation objects — presentation bootstraps itself.
- **Presentation** — 3D scene views, overlays, HUD, input raycasts, animations, audio playback. Subscribes to gameplay events; calls gameplay methods for actions.

## Communication pattern

```csharp
// Presentation sends input
GameState.Instance.PlayCard(cardId, color);

// Gameplay notifies presentation (subscribe in Start/OnEnable, unsubscribe in OnDestroy/OnDisable)
GameState.Instance.OnHandChanged.AddListener(RefreshHand);
```

Gameplay exposes data and events; presentation reads them in handlers. No callbacks from gameplay into presentation types.

## Namespaces match folders

Each project defines a namespace per assembly (e.g. `MT.<Game>.Runtime`, `MT.<Game>.Editor`). New scripts use the namespace matching their folder; no global-namespace scripts. `using` directives go outside the namespace block.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] New gameplay script under `Runtime/Gameplay/`, presentation under `Runtime/Presentation/`, generic utility under `Runtime/Core/`
- [ ] No presentation type references from Gameplay or Core
- [ ] Presentation communicates via gameplay events + command methods only
- [ ] Namespace matches the folder/assembly convention
